{"name":"Steelhead Redd","hex":"#d43728","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"steelhead","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The anadromous form of the rainbow trout, Oncorhynchus mykiss.","The ruddy duck."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/steelhead"},{"term":"redd","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To free from entanglement.","To free from embarrassment."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A spawning nest made by a fish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/redd"}],"entries":[{"title":"Steelhead","blocks":[{"text":"Steelhead, or occasionally steelhead trout, is the anadromous form of the coastal rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss irideus) or Columbia River redband trout. Steelhead are native to cold-water tributaries of the Pacific basin in Northeast Asia and North America. Like other sea-run (anadromous) trout and salmon, steelhead spawn in freshwater, smolts migrate to the ocean to forage for several years and adults return to their natal streams to spawn. Steelhead are iteroparous, although their survival rate is approximately only 10–20%."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steelhead"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Man_holding_a_steelhead_%28Oncorhynchus_mykiss%29_cropped.jpg/500px-Man_holding_a_steelhead_%28Oncorhynchus_mykiss%29_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Man_holding_a_steelhead_%28Oncorhynchus_mykiss%29_cropped.jpg/1280px-Man_holding_a_steelhead_%28Oncorhynchus_mykiss%29_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Man_holding_a_steelhead_%28Oncorhynchus_mykiss%29_cropped.jpg/500px-Man_holding_a_steelhead_%28Oncorhynchus_mykiss%29_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":